If you want to join the project and contribute, here are the improvements you can focus on:

1. Parallelize filesystem traversal using pthreads. Add a new CLI flag that lets the user specify the number of threads used to read data from multiple files concurrently. In this mode, each file’s hash must be computed in its own thread and therefore on its own CPU core (the OS should handle core scheduling). One file per thread, one core per file. This should achieve the maximum possible performance on storage that supports fast parallel access.

2. Add support for additional algorithms besides SHA512. SHA512 is fast and works well, but it does not support parallelism. The main algorithms of interest due to their speed and ability to parallelize are:
	a) XXH3 128 bit
	b) BLAKE3

3. Parallelize checksum computation for a single file using algorithms that support parallel hashing.

How it should work: imagine a large file. The program reads blocks of the file sequentially into an in memory buffer. If 10 CPU cores are available, the buffer is split into 10 parts and each part is hashed concurrently on different cores. Once hashing of the current buffer is finished, the next chunk of the file is read into the same buffer and the hashing step repeats. This approach uses all available CPU resources for checksumming while keeping disk IO single threaded, so it won’t degrade filesystem performance on slower storage. One file at a time for reading (no parallel reads), with many threads for checksum computation (CPU parallelism enabled).
